A||||3||skitime||Can mirror motors be removed without breaking anything.||||1||
Z||000000||skitime||03-30-2001||10:51 PM||||How do you remove a mirror motor. I want to paint the housing inside and out. Can it be removed without destroying anything? I was able to remove two of the three screws. The last one is behind the electrical plug.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||03-31-2001||02:57 AM||||Grab the mirror itself (not the housing) and rip it out. I'm serious. It comes out with no damage and pops right back in place. You may want to put the screws back before you pull the mirror out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||03-31-2001||04:26 AM||||Don't just pull on the mirror......you risk breaking it (I've broken a few this way!). <P>Instead, use a flat blade screwdriver to pop the mirror off of the motor assembly.  The glass is held in place by a 4-prong clip thing (as well as 2 screw-like things)....two of the prongs snap into the back of the glass while the other two attach to the motor.  It won't take much effort to remove and you will greatly reduce the risk of breaking your mirror glass.||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||04-02-2001||04:30 PM||||You can get to the mirror motor by removing the mirror but if you want to remove it out of the housing then you must remove the door panel and feed the wire up, disconnect the cable about 8" down and try to feed a 3/8" square connector through a small round hole. I have not been able to do it without pulling the mirror off the door. I shaved the edges of the connector with a utility knife to make it pull out.<BR>If you are just painting, pull the mirror and put a plastic baggie over the motor. The inside is only factory painted in about an inch anyway and aluminum will not rust if you miss some of it..||||reg||7
